(version 1)

# Custom design rules for Link to Video.
#
# These are the geometric half of the safety net. They cannot check that a pad
# is on the *correct* net -- DRC has no view of design intent, which is why the
# v1 emitter-to-rail fault passed both ERC and DRC cleanly. Net assignment is
# checked separately by tools/check_nets.py; run that too.
#
# What these rules do catch is the layout-adjacency version of the same
# mistake: the emitter node and the +5V rail drifting close enough together
# that a solder bridge, a whisker or an over-etched gap ties them. On v1 that
# short existed in copper and destroyed three transistors.

(rule "Q1 emitter node clear of the +5V rail"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.5mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == 'Net-(Q1-E)' && B.NetName == '/5V'")
)

(rule "Q1 emitter node clear of the fused rail"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.5mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == 'Net-(Q1-E)' && B.NetName == '/fused_5v'")
)

(rule "Q1 base node clear of the +5V rail"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.5mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == 'Net-(Q1-B)' && B.NetName == '/5V'")
)

# The two USB-C configuration channels are the other pair that must never meet.
# Bridging them puts 2.55k on CC instead of 5.1k, which falls below the source's
# vRd-Connect threshold -- the supply then never enables VBUS and the board looks
# completely dead, with no voltage and no current draw anywhere. This happened on
# the prototype.
#
# Note the pad exclusion. The receptacle's CC1 and CC2 are its two centre pads and
# sit on a fixed 0.3mm gap that no layout change can widen, so the rule would fire
# on the part itself. The prototype's bridge was solder across those pads -- an
# assembly defect DRC cannot prevent. What this rule does protect is the routing:
# CC2 runs 27mm across the board and CC1 15mm, and those are free to be spaced.

(rule "CC1 clear of CC2 (routing only)"
	(constraint clearance (min 0.4mm))
	(condition "A.NetName == 'Net-(USB-C1-CC1)' && B.NetName == 'Net-(USB-C1-CC2)' && A.Type != 'Pad' && B.Type != 'Pad'")
)
